1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,592
You checked your phone a few seconds
before you clicked this video.

2
00:00:04,920 --> 00:00:06,926
Maybe you ordered food.

3
00:00:07,276 --> 00:00:09,704
Maybe you asked an AI a question.

4
00:00:10,054 --> 00:00:13,706
Maybe you talked to someone on the other
side of the planet, instantly.

5
00:00:14,020 --> 00:00:18,030
We live in an age of artificial
intelligence, of rockets that land

6
00:00:17,880 --> 00:00:21,189
themselves, of a whole world that fits in
your pocket.

7
00:00:21,950 --> 00:00:23,132
And still...

8
00:00:23,732 --> 00:00:26,449
somewhere right now, there is a war.

9
00:00:26,799 --> 00:00:28,440
Russia and Ukraine.

10
00:00:28,710 --> 00:00:30,001
Israel and Gaza.

11
00:00:30,271 --> 00:00:32,266
Young men in trenches, again.

12
00:00:32,587 --> 00:00:33,941
Two hundred years.

13
00:00:34,211 --> 00:00:35,334
Four wars.

14
00:00:35,604 --> 00:00:37,148
Different weapons.

15
00:00:37,418 --> 00:00:38,814
The same story.

16
00:00:39,917 --> 00:00:40,695
Stop.

17
00:00:42,253 --> 00:00:44,584
Is it people who start wars...

18
00:00:45,184 --> 00:00:46,893
or is it human greed?

19
00:00:47,343 --> 00:00:48,849
Think about it for a second.

20
00:00:49,199 --> 00:00:52,362
Then tell me in the comments, I read every
single one.

21
00:00:53,920 --> 00:00:55,837
Today we're going back in time.

22
00:00:56,187 --> 00:01:00,397
We'll walk through three wars that changed
the world, we'll count what they cost, and

23
00:01:00,247 --> 00:01:03,692
then we'll ask one strange question: if
all those people had lived...

24
00:01:04,292 --> 00:01:06,569
how many of us would there be today?

25
00:01:07,019 --> 00:01:08,763
And could Earth even hold us?

26
00:01:10,050 --> 00:01:12,094
Summer, 1812.

27
00:01:12,364 --> 00:01:15,084
Europe belongs to one man: Napoleon
Bonaparte.

28
00:01:15,434 --> 00:01:17,551
He has beaten Austria.

29
00:01:17,901 --> 00:01:19,555
He has beaten Prussia.

30
00:01:19,905 --> 00:01:21,621
Kings are afraid of him.

31
00:01:21,971 --> 00:01:24,289
Only Russia still says no.

32
00:01:24,620 --> 00:01:28,642
So he gathers the largest army Europe has
ever seen.

33
00:01:28,992 --> 00:01:34,363
Frenchmen, Germans, Italians, Poles,
around six hundred thousand men, and tens

34
00:01:34,213 --> 00:01:35,902
of thousands of horses.

35
00:01:36,220 --> 00:01:40,808
On the twenty-fourth of June, at dawn,
they cross the Niemen river into Russia on

36
00:01:40,658 --> 00:01:41,684
pontoon bridges.

37
00:01:42,020 --> 00:01:44,094
Imagine standing there.

38
00:01:44,364 --> 00:01:45,528
No phones.

39
00:01:45,798 --> 00:01:47,020
No radio.

40
00:01:47,290 --> 00:01:48,314
No trucks.

41
00:01:48,584 --> 00:01:50,855
Orders travel on horseback.

42
00:01:51,205 --> 00:01:54,874
Bread travels on carts, and the carts are
always behind.

43
00:01:55,187 --> 00:01:55,955
They walk.

44
00:01:56,225 --> 00:02:01,019
Hundreds of kilometres, in the summer
heat, through empty villages.

45
00:02:01,369 --> 00:02:05,658
Because the Russians keep retreating, and
they burn the fields and the food they

46
00:02:05,508 --> 00:02:06,331
leave behind.

47
00:02:06,653 --> 00:02:09,443
Napoleon wanted one big battle, fast.

48
00:02:09,793 --> 00:02:11,667
Russia gave him distance instead.

49
00:02:12,017 --> 00:02:14,197
Smolensk burns in August.

50
00:02:14,547 --> 00:02:16,203
Still no victory.

51
00:02:17,067 --> 00:02:21,769
Finally, on the seventh of September, near
a small village called Borodino, the two

52
00:02:21,619 --> 00:02:22,427
armies meet.

53
00:02:23,087 --> 00:02:25,529
Cannon fire from morning until night.

54
00:02:25,879 --> 00:02:29,786
In a single day, tens of thousands of men
fall on both sides.

55
00:02:30,136 --> 00:02:33,730
It is one of the bloodiest days the world
had ever seen.

56
00:02:34,483 --> 00:02:37,715
A week later, Napoleon rides into Moscow.

57
00:02:38,065 --> 00:02:40,512
He expects the Tsar to send a messenger.

58
00:02:40,862 --> 00:02:41,988
To surrender.

59
00:02:43,100 --> 00:02:44,570
Instead...

60
00:02:45,170 --> 00:02:46,449
Moscow burns.

61
00:02:47,620 --> 00:02:50,031
Three quarters of the city is gone.

62
00:02:50,381 --> 00:02:52,065
No surrender.

63
00:02:52,335 --> 00:02:53,411
No food.

64
00:02:53,681 --> 00:02:55,533
No shelter for winter.

65
00:02:55,883 --> 00:02:59,374
Napoleon waits five weeks for a letter
that never comes.

66
00:02:59,687 --> 00:03:02,111
In October, he gives the order to leave.

67
00:03:02,850 --> 00:03:04,470
Then the winter arrives.

68
00:03:04,820 --> 00:03:06,285
Minus twenty.

69
00:03:06,555 --> 00:03:07,842
Minus thirty.

70
00:03:08,112 --> 00:03:09,903
Men freeze in their sleep.

71
00:03:10,220 --> 00:03:14,868
Horses die by the thousands, and soldiers
eat them to survive.

72
00:03:15,187 --> 00:03:20,465
At the end of November, at the Berezina
river, what is left of the army crosses

73
00:03:20,315 --> 00:03:25,658
under fire, on bridges built by engineers
standing chest-deep in freezing water.

74
00:03:25,987 --> 00:03:30,897
Of roughly six hundred thousand men who
marched into Russia...

75
00:03:31,497 --> 00:03:33,707
only about one hundred thousand came back.

76
00:03:35,017 --> 00:03:36,560
Now think about it.

77
00:03:36,910 --> 00:03:42,726
If you were Napoleon, standing at that
river in June, with the whole of Europe

78
00:03:42,576 --> 00:03:45,487
behind you, would you have crossed it?

79
00:03:47,253 --> 00:03:49,132
Quick thing before we go on.

80
00:03:49,482 --> 00:03:53,662
I make these videos on my own, and I
really want to keep making them.

81
00:03:54,012 --> 00:03:59,143
If you're enjoying this story, subscribing
and hitting like costs you nothing, but

82
00:03:58,993 --> 00:04:02,485
for a small channel like this one, it
means everything.

83
00:04:03,483 --> 00:04:08,778
One hundred years later, Europe is richer,
faster, and more connected than ever.

84
00:04:09,128 --> 00:04:10,081
Railways.

85
00:04:10,351 --> 00:04:11,320
Telegraph.

86
00:04:11,590 --> 00:04:12,561
Factories.

87
00:04:12,831 --> 00:04:16,262
People believe a big war in Europe is now
impossible.

88
00:04:16,612 --> 00:04:18,394
It would be too expensive.

89
00:04:18,744 --> 00:04:19,895
Too stupid.

90
00:04:20,220 --> 00:04:25,843
Then, on the twenty-eighth of June, 1914,
one man fires a pistol in Sarajevo, and

91
00:04:25,693 --> 00:04:28,952
kills the heir to the Austro-Hungarian
throne.

92
00:04:29,287 --> 00:04:34,235
Within six weeks, alliance after alliance
pulls almost all of Europe into war.

93
00:04:34,585 --> 00:04:37,746
Everyone says it will be over by
Christmas.

94
00:04:38,053 --> 00:04:39,513
It is not.

95
00:04:39,784 --> 00:04:41,050
Machine guns.

96
00:04:41,320 --> 00:04:42,984
Barbed wire.

97
00:04:43,254 --> 00:04:44,593
Poison gas.

98
00:04:44,863 --> 00:04:49,302
Millions of men living in trenches,
sometimes only a few hundred metres from

99
00:04:49,152 --> 00:04:49,979
their enemy.

100
00:04:50,287 --> 00:04:53,431
They write letters home by candlelight.

101
00:04:53,781 --> 00:04:58,358
Many of those letters arrive after the men
who wrote them are already dead.

102
00:04:58,687 --> 00:05:01,631
For four years, the front line barely
moves.

103
00:05:01,981 --> 00:05:07,426
By 1918, between fifteen and twenty-two
million people are dead, soldiers and

104
00:05:07,276 --> 00:05:08,113
civilians.

105
00:05:09,117 --> 00:05:10,581
One gunshot.

106
00:05:10,851 --> 00:05:13,069
Twenty million lives.

107
00:05:14,253 --> 00:05:18,730
If you could go back and stop one decision
in the summer of 1914...

108
00:05:19,330 --> 00:05:20,673
which one would it be?

109
00:05:22,883 --> 00:05:25,799
Just twenty-one years later, the world is
at war again.

110
00:05:26,149 --> 00:05:30,092
By 1941, Germany controls most of Europe.

111
00:05:30,442 --> 00:05:33,751
And Hitler makes the same choice Napoleon
made.

112
00:05:34,087 --> 00:05:38,832
On the twenty-second of June, 1941, almost
the same week Napoleon crossed the Niemen,

113
00:05:38,682 --> 00:05:40,616
Germany invades the Soviet Union.

114
00:05:40,966 --> 00:05:43,328
Around three point eight million men.

115
00:05:43,678 --> 00:05:46,233
The largest invasion in human history.

116
00:05:46,553 --> 00:05:49,713
This time there are tanks, planes and
radios.

117
00:05:50,063 --> 00:05:53,168
The army moves faster than any army before
it.

118
00:05:53,518 --> 00:05:55,704
But the land is just as big.

119
00:05:56,054 --> 00:05:58,148
And the winter is just as cold.

120
00:05:58,883 --> 00:06:03,830
By December, German soldiers can see the
outskirts of Moscow, and once again, the

121
00:06:03,680 --> 00:06:05,724
cold and the Red Army stop them.

122
00:06:06,053 --> 00:06:09,248
The war in the East lasts four more years.

123
00:06:09,598 --> 00:06:13,963
Cities like Leningrad and Stalingrad
become names the world never forgets.

124
00:06:14,320 --> 00:06:17,770
The Soviet Union alone lost about twenty-
seven million people.

125
00:06:18,087 --> 00:06:22,575
In the whole Second World War, between
seventy and eighty-five million people

126
00:06:22,425 --> 00:06:22,857
died.

127
00:06:23,207 --> 00:06:26,458
About three percent of everyone alive on
Earth.

128
00:06:27,850 --> 00:06:29,293
So let's do the math.

129
00:06:29,643 --> 00:06:33,029
Not exact, nobody can know that, but a
simple, honest estimate.

130
00:06:33,353 --> 00:06:37,220
In 1918 there were about one point eight
billion people on Earth.

131
00:06:37,570 --> 00:06:41,823
Today there are about eight point two
billion, about four and a half times more.

132
00:06:42,153 --> 00:06:46,981
So the twenty million lost in the First
World War could have become roughly ninety

133
00:06:46,831 --> 00:06:48,521
million people alive today.

134
00:06:48,853 --> 00:06:51,957
The seventy to eighty-five million lost in
the Second World War?

135
00:06:52,407 --> 00:06:55,037
Multiply by about three and a half...

136
00:06:55,637 --> 00:06:58,788
roughly two hundred and fifty to three
hundred million.

137
00:06:59,120 --> 00:07:03,160
Add Napoleon's war, and we get somewhere
around three hundred and fifty to four

138
00:07:03,010 --> 00:07:06,312
hundred million people who are missing
from the world right now.

139
00:07:06,620 --> 00:07:10,063
That's more than the entire population of
the United States.

140
00:07:11,020 --> 00:07:12,553
Could Earth hold them?

141
00:07:13,003 --> 00:07:14,030
Yes.

142
00:07:14,300 --> 00:07:15,111
Easily.

143
00:07:15,381 --> 00:07:17,257
It's about five percent more people.

144
00:07:17,587 --> 00:07:20,167
So the problem was never space.

145
00:07:20,517 --> 00:07:21,648
Never food.

146
00:07:21,918 --> 00:07:23,070
Never land.

147
00:07:24,483 --> 00:07:25,771
1812.

148
00:07:26,041 --> 00:07:27,505
1914.

149
00:07:27,775 --> 00:07:29,078
1941.

150
00:07:29,348 --> 00:07:30,291
Today.

151
00:07:30,561 --> 00:07:33,396
Every time, someone believed the war would
be quick.

152
00:07:33,746 --> 00:07:36,638
Every time, ordinary people paid the
price.

153
00:07:36,953 --> 00:07:38,928
So I'll ask you one more time.

154
00:07:39,278 --> 00:07:40,747
Is it people...

155
00:07:41,347 --> 00:07:42,623
or is it greed?

156
00:07:44,187 --> 00:07:48,595
And what's your guess, if none of these
wars had happened, what would our world

157
00:07:48,445 --> 00:07:49,457
look like today?

158
00:07:49,907 --> 00:07:52,096
Write it in the comments.

159
00:07:54,283 --> 00:07:57,239
If this video made you think, please
support the channel.

160
00:07:57,589 --> 00:08:02,127
Subscribe, leave a like, and send it to
one friend who loves history.

161
00:08:02,477 --> 00:08:06,478
It's free for you, and it's what keeps
these stories coming.

162
00:08:06,828 --> 00:08:08,516
Thank you for watching.

163
00:08:09,087 --> 00:08:14,183
And next time: how Christopher Columbus
changed two continents, forever.
